# Why the programme is described as unconditional

In standard mathematical usage, a proof is called unconditional when it does not rely on open conjectures.

The programme documented here is presented in that sense:
- it does not rely on the former “Hypothesis 1.1”;
- it instead invokes established results from the literature plus bridge arguments.

The existence of `axiom` declarations in Lean does not by itself mean that the mathematical argument depends on conjectures.
It means that some external published results have not yet been fully transcribed into Lean/Mathlib.
